11	/**
12	 * Resize as a slider across the real catalogue, not a wall of plan cards.
13	 *
14	 * WHY A SLIDER AND NOT FOUR OF THEM. The concept draws separate CPU, Memory,
15	 * Storage and Bandwidth sliders, each priced independently. On this platform
16	 * those four do not move independently — a plan is a fixed bundle, and resize
17	 * targets a plan id. Four sliders would imply you can buy 6 vCPU against 8 GB,
18	 * which you cannot, and the Apply button would have to quietly snap you to the
19	 * nearest plan. So there is ONE track whose stops are the plans that exist, and
20	 * each stop states everything it changes. Storage genuinely IS independent and
21	 * keeps its own control, next door.
22	 *
23	 * Every figure comes from the same functions the server charges through —
24	 * prorateUpgradeCents and walletSplit, exactly as ResizePicker used them. The
25	 * interaction is new; the money is not re-derived.
26	 */
27	
28	type PlanRow = {
29	  id: string;
30	  name: string;
31	  cpuCores: number;
32	  ramMB: number;
33	  diskGB: number;
34	  bandwidthTB: number;
35	  priceCents: number;
36	};
