/* Liquid glass, rebuilt a third time, this pass on the owner's own explicit
   spec (two written prompts with exact rgba values) rather than on a
   measurement or a guess. Two things changed from every earlier version:
   the fill is a real translucent NAVY now, not transparent (the "no colour
   at all" idea from several passes back is retired — the owner asked for a
   filled glass surface this time, explicitly), and there is NO outer glow
   anywhere — the previous pass's colour-mix'd box-shadow bloom bleeding
   into the page is gone. "Only inner glow, no outer glow" and "the radius
   is too big" were both direct corrections; both are addressed in
   `::before` below rather than argued with.

   `::after` and its mask-composite ring are gone too — the spec calls for
   a plain flat single-alpha border, which the native `border` property
   already does correctly (and, unlike the ring hack, respects
   border-radius by construction, so there is nothing left to get wrong
   about corners). One real element, one pseudo-element. */
.card-glass {
  --tone: #50b4ff;   /* default: blue accent. Fill stays the same dark navy
                         on every card — only this edge accent varies per
                         instance, set inline (RewardsView.tsx). */
  position: relative;
  overflow: hidden;   /* guarantees ::before's glow — blurred, so it can
                         technically paint past its own pseudo-element box —
                         never escapes the card's rounded edge. Structural,
                         not a per-instance convention: some Panels were
                         relying on their OWN className carrying
                         overflow-hidden, and not all of them did. */
  /* No colour fill at all — a real glass PANE, not a tinted panel. Two
     earlier attempts (0.55, then 0.30 navy) both still read as "a dark
     card" rather than "glass", and disabling blur entirely to check
     showed why: with any fill on top of it, blurring what little sits
     behind these cards just muddies it further. A genuinely transparent
     fill plus blur is what actually reads as a pane you're looking
     through, not a coloured surface with a blur filter on it. */
  background: transparent;
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--tone) 35%, transparent);
  box-shadow: 0 12px 28px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35);   /* plain lift — no colour, no glow */
